  "articleBody": "It's a common homeowner headache: you've installed new trim, and instead of crisp, clean lines, you're left with noticeable gaps and unevenness because your walls aren't perfectly straight. Don't sweat it too much—it happens all the time, especially in older homes! Walls and floors rarely come perfectly plumb, level, or square, and if you don't account for these imperfections during installation, your trim will definitely highlight them. The main culprits are often poor miter cuts that don't quite meet up, or simply not checking for level and plumb as you go. Forgetting to acclimate wood trim to your home's humidity can also lead to warping and gapping down the line. The solution often involves careful measurements, precise cuts, and a lot of shimming and scribing to custom-fit the trim to the wall's actual contours. After fastening, filling nail holes and caulking seams are critical steps to achieve that smooth, professional finish. If you're facing a room with many corners, custom profiles, or significant wall irregularities, getting a professional involved is a smart move. Experts have the skills to handle those challenging conditions, ensuring seamless joints and a consistent look that hides any wall imperfections beautifully. They'll make sure your trim looks like it was always meant to be there, no matter how wonky your walls might be!",
